  "title": "Module 07 opener — Hormones and Peptides for Skin",
  "note": "Module opener in the cinematic lane (docs/motion-contract.md § 10b), same look and rules as the Module 1 opener. Script assembled only from approved text (sources below); presenter lines pass the course script review; draft media until the media preview.",
  "script": {
    "text": "This last module turns the course into three habits. You will rate the certainty behind a claim with GRADE, read a compounding status and a certificate of analysis for what they do and do not prove, and write the consent and chart note that an unapproved product needs. Every claim you hear about a skin peptide carries a hidden question. How sure can we be? GRADE answers it with four levels of certainty, from high to very low. The rating belongs to a body of evidence for one outcome, such as wrinkle depth at twelve weeks, and never to a single study or to the molecule itself. Randomized trials start high and observational studies start low. From there, certainty drops for five reasons you can name, which are risk of bias, inconsistency between trials, indirectness, imprecision, and suspected publication bias. Both Hormonaly guides print a letter from A to D beside each use. Read those letters as the same four levels, adapted. They rate the evidence for that use, and they say nothing about whether a product is legal.",
    "sources": [
      "m7-p01 body, sentences 1, 2",
      "m7-p02 audioScript, sentences 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10"
    ],
